<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> the best ski goggles fit your face and helmet with no gap, have a lens tint that matches the light you usually ski in, and resist fogging. If you ski in both sun and storms, choose goggles with interchangeable lenses or carry a second low-light lens.</p></div>

<h2>Why goggles matter</h2>
<p>Goggles protect your eyes from wind, cold, snow, and UV, which is stronger at altitude and reflects off snow. Most importantly, the right lens helps you see the shape of the snow, which makes skiing easier and safer, especially in flat light.</p>

<h2>Lens tint and VLT</h2>
<p>Lenses are rated by visible light transmission (VLT), the percentage of light that passes through.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Low VLT (darker, roughly under 20%)</strong>: bright, sunny days.</li>
  <li><strong>Mid VLT (roughly 20–40%)</strong>: mixed sun and clouds; the most versatile single lens.</li>
  <li><strong>High VLT (lighter, roughly over 40%)</strong>: storms, flat light, and night skiing. Yellow, rose, and amber tints often boost contrast.</li>
</ul>
<p>Check the <%= link_to "snow forecast", where_is_it_snowing_path %> before a trip: a stormy week means you'll want a high-VLT lens.</p>

<h2>Lens types</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Interchangeable lenses</strong> let you swap a sunny lens for a low-light one. Magnetic systems are the quickest to change. See <%= gear_link "ski goggles with interchangeable lenses", "ski goggles interchangeable lens" %>.</li>
  <li><strong>Photochromic lenses</strong> darken and lighten with conditions, which is convenient if you don't want to swap.</li>
  <li><strong>Polarized lenses</strong> cut glare but can make icy patches harder to read; preferences vary.</li>
  <li><strong>Lens shape:</strong> spherical lenses curve both ways for a wider view and less distortion; cylindrical lenses are flat vertically and often cost less. Both work well.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Preventing fog</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Look for <strong>double lenses</strong> and an <strong>anti-fog coating</strong> on the inside.</li>
  <li>Good <strong>venting</strong> along the top of the frame keeps air moving.</li>
  <li>Don't wipe the inside of the lens; it can remove the anti-fog coating. Let it air dry.</li>
  <li>Don't push goggles up onto your forehead, where they trap sweat and steam.</li>
  <li>Make sure your helmet doesn't block the goggle vents.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Fit</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Face fit:</strong> foam should seal evenly with no gaps, especially at the nose and cheeks.</li>
  <li><strong>Helmet fit:</strong> there should be no gap between the top of the goggles and your helmet (sometimes called "gaper gap"). Try them together if you can. See the <%= link_to "ski helmet guide", guide_path("best-ski-helmet") %>.</li>
  <li><strong>Frame size:</strong> goggles come in small, medium, and large frames. Smaller faces and kids need smaller frames.</li>
  <li><strong>Over-the-glasses (OTG):</strong> if you wear glasses, choose <%= gear_link "OTG ski goggles", "OTG ski goggles" %> designed with room for frames.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Picks by situation</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Mostly sunny resorts:</strong> a darker main lens, plus a spare low-light lens for storm days.</li>
  <li><strong>Storm chasing and powder days:</strong> a high-contrast, high-VLT lens.</li>
  <li><strong>One lens only:</strong> a mid-VLT or photochromic lens.</li>
  <li><strong>Kids:</strong> a small frame that fits their helmet, with a mid-VLT lens.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Care</h2>
<p>Store goggles in their soft bag, which doubles as a lens cloth for the outside only. Dry them open at room temperature, not on a heater.</p>
<p>To stop goggles fogging from your breath, keep your face cover below your nose or use a balaclava with a breathable mouth panel. More in <%= link_to "balaclava vs ski mask vs neck gaiter", guide_path("balaclava-vs-ski-mask") %>.</p>

<% faqs << { question: "What color ski goggle lens is best?", answer: "It depends on light. Darker, low-VLT lenses suit sunny days, and lighter yellow, rose, or amber high-VLT lenses suit storms and flat light. A mid-VLT lens is the best all-around single choice." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "How do I stop my ski goggles from fogging?", answer: "Choose double, vented lenses with an anti-fog coating, don't wipe the inside of the lens, avoid pushing goggles onto your forehead, and make sure your helmet doesn't block the vents." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "Can I wear glasses under ski goggles?", answer: "Yes, with OTG (over-the-glasses) goggles that have room in the frame for eyeglasses." } %>
